<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pashupata Shaivism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pashupata</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pashupata <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a> is the oldest named Shaiva sect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, traditionally founded by the teacher Lakulisha in the early centuries CE. Its ascetics worshipped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> as Pashupati, lord of creatures, and practised unconventional behaviour to court contempt and destroy pride. The sect was influential across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the first millennium and was patronised by dynasties such as the Kalachuris of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahishmati">Mahishmati</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pashupata-shaivism.jpg" alt="Pashupata Shaivism" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vidaikodiselvar S. Danabala · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SaivismFlag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
